Title: The Velveteen Rabbit

Credit: Written by  
Author: Margery Williams  
Source: Adapted from book  
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A cozy nursery filled with toys. A VELVETEEN RABBIT, fat and bunchy, sits at the top of a BOY'S STOCKING, a sprig of holly between his paws. 

The BOY, around five, bursts into the room, eyes wide with excitement.

BOY  
(gleeful)  
Look! A rabbit!

The BOY grabs the RABBIT and hugs him tightly. 

NARRATOR  
Here was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

The BOY plays with the RABBIT, but soon AUNTS and UNCLES arrive, causing a flurry of unwrapping gifts. The RABBIT is forgotten.

INT. TOY CUPBOARD - LATER

The RABBIT sits alone in the toy cupboard, looking sad. 

NARRATOR  
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor, and no one thought very much about him.

The mechanical toys boast and swagger around him, looking down on the RABBIT.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The SKIN HORSE, old and wise, lies beside the RABBIT.

RABBIT  
What is REAL?

SKIN HORSE  
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time... then you become Real.

RABBIT  
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE  
Sometimes. When you are Real, you don't mind being hurt.

RABBIT  
Does it happen all at once?

SKIN HORSE  
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The BOY is tucked in bed, holding the RABBIT tightly. 

NARRATOR  
That night, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

The RABBIT smiles, feeling loved.

EXT. GARDEN - SPRING TIME

The BOY and the RABBIT play outside, having picnics and adventures. 

NARRATOR  
Spring came, and they had long days in the garden.

The BOY leaves the RABBIT outside one evening. The RABBIT gets wet from the dew.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The BOY is in bed, looking at the RABBIT.

BOY  
Give me my Bunny! He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The RABBIT beams with joy.

NARRATOR  
The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was Real.

EXT. WOOD - SUMMER DAYS

The RABBIT lies in the grass, watching ants. Two wild rabbits appear, curious and new.

WILD RABBIT  
Why don't you get up and play with us?

RABBIT  
I don't feel like it.

The wild rabbits laugh at him.

WILD RABBIT  
He hasn't got any hind legs!

The RABBIT feels ashamed.

RABBIT  
I am Real! The Boy said so!

The wild rabbits run away. The RABBIT is left alone.

NARRATOR  
Oh, dear! Why did they run away like that?

EXT. GARDEN - NIGHT

The RABBIT lies in the corner, feeling lonely. A tear falls from his nose, and a magical flower grows.

NARRATOR  
And then a strange thing happened.

A FAIRY appears from the flower, radiant and kind.

FAIRY  
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am? I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

RABBIT  
Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY  
You were Real to the Boy. Now you shall be Real to everyone.

The FAIRY kisses the RABBIT and he transforms.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

The RABBIT, now with hind legs and soft fur, jumps joyfully among the wild rabbits.

NARRATOR  
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - SPRING

The BOY plays in the wood, spotting two rabbits. One looks familiar.

BOY  
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost!

The RABBIT watches, a knowing look in his eyes.

NARRATOR  
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

FADE OUT.
